get PCA and EOF and how you use them with input eg T

Sure — here's the conceptual walkthrough, tied directly to your code.

The physical idea

A temperature profile is 100 numbers, but it doesn't wander freely
through 100-dimensional space — real atmospheric temperature profiles
are constrained by physics (hydrostatic balance, radiative-convective
equilibrium, dynamics), so they cluster near a much lower-dimensional
surface within that 100-D space. PCA finds the directions along which
your actual sample of profiles varies the most, and lets you describe
each profile as a mean shape plus a short list of coefficients along
those directions, instead of 100 independent numbers.

In atmospheric science this exact technique is called EOF analysis
(Empirical Orthogonal Functions) — same math as PCA, different
name/tradition. "EOF" = the basis vector (a shape across the 100
levels); "PC score" or "coefficient" = how much of that shape a given
profile contains.

The math, briefly : Given your training matrix T (nSamples × 100 levels):

1) Mean profile: mu = mean(T, 1) — the average temperature at each
  level across all your samples. This is the "shape everyone shares."
  
2) Anomalies: T - mu — how each individual profile deviates from that
  mean, level by level.

3) Covariance structure: PCA finds the directions (eigenvectors of the
  covariance matrix of the anomalies) along which those deviations
  have the most variance. The first eigenvector is the single shape
  that, when scaled up or down, best explains the spread across your
  whole training set. The second is the next-best shape orthogonal to
  the first, and so on.

4) Each profile can then be written as: T_i ≈ mu + c1*EOF1 + c2*EOF2 + ... + cK*EOFK
  where EOF1, EOF2, ... are the fixed basis shapes (same for every
  profile) and c1, c2, ... are that profile's own coefficients
  (different per profile). Keep only the first K components (K=12 for
  T, in your script) because the later ones contribute almost nothing
  — this is exactly the "vertical structure is low-rank" observation
  from earlier in this conversation.

>>>
Walking through fitSpectralPCA

function S = fitSpectralPCA(data, nComponents)
    [coeff, score, ~, ~, explained, mu] = pca(data, 'NumComponents', nComponents);
    S.coeff = coeff;                          % (nDim x nComponents)
    S.mu    = mu;                             % (1 x nDim)
    S.coeffMean = mean(score,1);
    S.coeffStd  = std(score,0,1) + 1e-12;
    S.explainedVarRatio = explained/100;

  data here is T (nSamples × 100) — MATLAB's pca automatically
  computes the mean and subtracts it internally, this is not something
  you do by hand.

  coeff — the EOF basis vectors themselves, one column per component,
  so coeff(:,1) is EOF1 (a 100-long shape), coeff(:,4) is EOF4 (the
  one you were just investigating), etc. This is the same object as
  "eigenvectors of the covariance matrix" above.

  mu — the mean profile, (1 × 100), i.e. step 1 above.

  score — this is c1, c2, ..., cK for every training profile at once:
  (nSamples × nComponents). Row i, column 4 is exactly the "Q PC4
  score for profile i" you were plotting a couple messages ago.

  explained — percent of total variance each component accounts for,
  in order. This is what fed your cum_var/n_needed checks earlier —
  how many components you actually need to hit e.g. 99.99%.

  coeffMean/coeffStd — precomputed so you can standardize coefficients
  later if you choose to (you tried this and found it hurt, since it
  over-weights near-noise components — see a few messages back).

>>>
encodeSpectral — going from a profile to its coefficients

function coeffs = encodeSpectral(S, data, standardize)
    coeffs = (data - S.mu) * S.coeff;

This is literally step 2 + step 4 solved for the c's: subtract the
mean (get the anomaly), then project onto each EOF direction via a dot
product (matrix multiply does all components at once). (data - S.mu)
is (nSamples × 100), S.coeff is (100 × nComponents), so the product is
(nSamples × nComponents) — one row of coefficients per profile. This
is what produces tEOF, qScores, o3Scores in your script.

>>>
decodeSpectral — going back from coefficients to a full profile

function data = decodeSpectral(S, coeffs, standardize)
    data = coeffs * S.coeff' + S.mu;

The exact inverse: coeffs * S.coeff' reconstructs the anomaly (undoes
the projection), then + S.mu adds the mean back to get an actual
profile in physical units again. This is what your truncation-floor
check used — encode then immediately decode, with no network involved,
to measure how much information is lost purely by keeping only K
components.

>>>
How this connects to T specifically in your pipeline

  tPCA = fitSpectralPCA(T, nPCA_T);          % learn the 12 EOF shapes + mean, from your 38k T profiles
  tEOF = encodeSpectral(tPCA, T, true);       % compress every profile to 12 numbers each

tEOF — (nSamples × 12) — is what actually goes into your NN network's
input feature vector X, replacing the raw 100-level T array. The
network never sees the full profile; it sees "how much of EOF-shape-1,
how much of EOF-shape-2, ..." for each profile, which is a far more
compact, physically-organized description of the same information
(assuming 12 components capture enough variance — that's what your
explainedVarRatio printout checks).

The same exact function serves three different roles in your script
depending on what you feed it: T/Q_log/O3_log (input profile
compression), emissivityRaw (input spectral compression), and btRaw
(output/target compression) — it's the same math applied to three
physically different quantities, which is why it was worth writing
once as a generic, reusable function rather than three separate ones.

