So fitSpectralPCA takes matrix T and finds mu, and then the PCs and the means/std
After which you profject T onto the PCA to get the EOF scores?

Yes, exactly right on both counts. To restate it precisely with your terms:
  fitSpectralPCA(T, nPCA_T) does the "learning" step, once:
  
  Computes mu — the mean profile across all your training samples.
   
  Finds the PCs (coeff) — the eigenvectors of the covariance of the
  mean-subtracted data, i.e. the fixed set of basis shapes.

  Also computes coeffMean/coeffStd — but note these are the mean and
  std of the scores themselves (computed by projecting the training
  set once internally, via score from MATLAB's pca), not something
  separate from the projection step. They exist so you could
  standardize coefficients later, not because they're needed to define
  the PCs.

encodeSpectral(tPCA, T, ...) does the "projection" step, applied to any data:

  This is exactly what you called it: projecting T onto the PCs found
  above, i.e. (T - mu) * coeff, which gives you the EOF scores — one
  row of nPCA_T numbers per profile.

One nuance worth being precise about since you're clearly building
solid intuition here: the projection step (encodeSpectral) isn't
limited to the same data used to fit the PCs. You fit tPCA once on
your training set, but then you call encodeSpectral(tPCA, ...) on
validation data too, using the training-set PCs and mean — you never
refit PCA on validation data. That's the same "fit only on train,
never on val/test" principle from way back when we first discussed the
ProfileNormalizer. If you accidentally refit fitSpectralPCA on
validation data separately, you'd get a different basis (different
coeff, different mu) than the one the network was trained against, and
encodeSpectral/decodeSpectral would silently give you
scores/reconstructions in an inconsistent coordinate system — worth
double-checking that's not happening anywhere in your current
pipeline, since it's a very easy mistake to make once you're juggling
multiple PCA structs (tPCA, qPCA, o3PCA, emisPCA, btPCA) across a
growing script.

