FOr the profiles and emissivity, I call encodeSpectral with
sttandardize='true' but for BT it is "false"

That's the right call, and it's worth being clear on why it's
different from the BT target case, since you already saw standardizing
hurt there — the two situations aren't analogous, even though it's the
same function.

For BT (the target/output): standardizing forced the loss to weight
every PCA component equally, including the late, mostly-noise
components. That directly shapes what the optimizer is told to
prioritize — equal weighting means the network spends effort chasing
noise in weak components at the expense of the dominant ones. That's a
loss-function problem specific to something being optimized against.

For T/Q/O3 (inputs): there's no loss being computed on these values —
they're just features going into the first layer. Standardizing here
is the same ordinary reason you already z-score
surfTemp/surfPressure/viewAngleSecant: gradient-based optimizers work
better when input features sit on comparable scales, so no single
feature dominates the early gradients purely because it happens to
have larger raw units (EOF1 for temperature naturally has much larger
magnitude than EOF10, purely because it captures more variance —
without standardizing, the optimizer would implicitly treat EOF1 as
"more important" at initialization for reasons that have nothing to do
with its actual predictive value). Standardizing puts all 12 T
components, all 15 Q components, etc., on equal footing as inputs,
which is a different concern than weighting them equally in a
loss. There's no equivalent "wasted capacity chasing noise" risk on
the input side — the network is free to learn small weights for
less-useful input features if that's what the data supports,
standardizing just makes sure it isn't forced to learn artificially
large ones first just to compensate for scale.

So: standardize=true for T/Q/O3/emissivity (inputs) and
standardize=false for BT (target) is the correct, consistent pattern —
not an inconsistency, two different roles for the same function.

One thing worth double-checking given the pattern of bugs you've been
finding: make sure decodeSpectral calls (if you ever reconstruct
T/Q/O3 profiles for visualization/debugging, like the
flagged-vs-normal plot from earlier) use the same standardize flag as
the corresponding encodeSpectral call that produced those coefficients
— mismatched flags there would silently reconstruct garbage, same
failure mode as the earlier decodeSpectral(btPCA, predCoeffsVal, true)
vs false mismatch discussion.
